# ── LF IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE HERE — IT IS A DEPLOY PRECONDITION ─────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (audit, 2026-08-04):
# 293 of 323 migrations were CRLF in the Windows working tree while LF in the index, because
# core.autocrlf=true and nothing pinned them. That is not cosmetic. Migrations since 0299 rewrite
# deployed function bodies by SLICING them: a literal `old_t` must match text returned by
# pg_get_functiondef(), and Postgres returns LF-only. A CRLF `old_t` matches ZERO occurrences, the
# migration's own guard raises "guard text occurs 0 time(s)", and the whole deploy rolls back.
#
# It fails loudly rather than silently, which is the one mercy — and it only bites a local
# `supabase db push` from Windows, because CI checks out the LF index and never sees it. That is
# exactly why it survived: the machine that hits it is the machine with no CI to catch it.
#
# One line closes the entire class. It did not exist until now.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The generators read migration text and normalise on read, then git handed the file straight back
# as CRLF. Pin them for the same reason.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.sh  text eol=lf

# Everything else: normalise in the repo, let git decide the checkout.
* text=auto
